          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            a
            %em projective/injective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
            chain complex that consists of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+objects"} injective objects
          %p
            Under suitable conditions these are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+resolution"} cofibrant resolution
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolution"} fibrant resolution
            with respect to a standard
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
          %p
            For instance for non-negatively graded chain complexes of abelian groups there is a model structure with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are the quasi-isomorphisms and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            are the positive-degreewise surjections. Here every object is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+object"} fibrant object
            and hence no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolution"} fibrant resolution
            is necessary; while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+resolutions"} cofibrant resolutions
            are precisely the projective resolutions.
          %p
            Dually, for non-negatively graded chain complexes of abelian groups there is a model structure with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are the quasi-isomorphisms and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            the positive-degreewise injections. Here every object is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+object"} cofibrant object
            and hence no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+resolution"} cofibrant resolution
            is necessary; while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolutions"} fibrant resolutions
            are precisely the projective resolutions.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p We first discuss, as is traditional, projective/injective resolutions of single objects, and then the general cases of projective/injective resolutions of chain complexes. This subsumes the previous case by regarding an object as a chain complex concentrated in degree 0.
          %h3#ResolutionOfObject Resolution of an object
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
          #InjectiveResolution.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{A}
              an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %strong injective resolution
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^\bullet \in Ch^\bullet(\mathcal{A})
              (in non-negative degree) equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    i : X \stackrel{\sim}{\to} J^\bullet
            %p
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^n \in \mathcal{A}
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
          #InjectiveResolutionInComponents.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In components the quasi-isomorphism of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InjectiveResolution"}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mi i
                                    %mn 0
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi d
                                  %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{i^0}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    J^0 &amp;\stackrel{d^0}{\to}&amp; J^1 &amp;\stackrel{d^1}{\to}&amp; \cdots &amp;\to&amp;
                    J^n &amp;\stackrel{d^n}{\to}&amp;\cdots
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since the top complex is concentrated in degree 0, this being a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              happens to be equivalent to the sequence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi d
                          %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi d
                          %mn 2
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to X \stackrel{i^0}{\to} J^0 \stackrel{d^0}{\to} J^1 \stackrel{d^1}{\to}
                    J^2 \stackrel{d^2}{\to}
                    \\cdots
            %p
              being an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
          #ProjectiveResolution.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{A}
              an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %strong projective resolution
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_\bullet \in Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
              (in non-negative degree) equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p : J_\bullet \stackrel{\sim}{\to} X
            %p
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_n \in \mathcal{A}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
          #ProjectiveResolutionInComponents.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In components the quasi-isomorphism of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProjectiveResolution"}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi J
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi n
                                    %mo −
                                    %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi J
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi J
                              %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi p
                                    %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\cdots &amp;\stackrel{\partial_n}{\to}&amp; J_n &amp;\stackrel{\partial_{n-1}}{\to}&amp;
                    \\cdots &amp;\to&amp; J_1 &amp;\stackrel{\partial_0}{\to}&amp; J_0
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{p_0}}
                    \\\
                    \\cdots &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since the bottom complex is concentrated in degree 0, this being a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              happens to be equivalent to the sequence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cdots J_2 \stackrel{\partial_1}{\to} J_1 \stackrel{\partial_0}{\to} J_0 \stackrel{p_0}{\to}
                    X \to 0
            %p
              being an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
          %h3#FResolutions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            \-Resolutions of an object
          %p
            Projective and injective resolutions are typically used for computing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor+in+homological+algebra"} derived functor in homological algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            are
            %em sufficient
            for computing
            %em every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+derived+functor"} left derived functor
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
            and injective resolutions are sufficient for computing
            %em every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+derived+functor"} right derived functor
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^\bullet(\mathcal{A})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            then such resolutions may be more than
            = succeed "." do
              %em necessary
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              \-projective resolutions
            and
            = succeed "," do
